---
source: tests/stages.rs
expression: reports.report(path)
input_file: tests/fixtures/expand/doc_vec_pages.knv
---
(def vec-page "(defmacro vec (elements ...))\n\nBuilds a vector from its elements, which all have one type.\n\n`(vec)` is empty. `vec` is also the type `(vec a)`, Rust's `Vec<A>`.\n\n(len (vec 1 2 3))\n=> 3\n\n(empty? (the (vec int) (vec)))\n=> true")
(def iter-page "(iter collection)\n\nReturns an iterator over borrows of a collection's items.\n\nA vector or a slice yields `(ref a)`, and a map yields\n`(pair (ref key) (ref value))`. Where a call expects an [iterator], a vector,\na slice or a map passes without `iter`.\n\n(-> (vec 1 2) iter (map str) collect)\n=> (vec (str 1) (str 2))")
(def sort-page "(sort! items)\n\nSorts a vector in place, in ascending order.\n\n`(sort! items <)` does the same, and `(sort! items >)` sorts in descending\norder. Both need items that implement [ord].\n\n`(sort! items compare)` orders by a function of two `(ref a)` that returns\nan [int]. A negative result puts the first item first, a positive result\nputs it last, and zero keeps the two in order. Rust's `sort_by` may panic\nwhen `compare` is not a consistent order.\n\n(let (items (vec 3 1 2))\n  (sort! items)\n  items)\n=> (vec 1 2 3)\n\n(let (items (vec 3 1 2))\n  (sort! items >)\n  items)\n=> (vec 3 2 1)")
(def windows-page "(windows items size)\n\nReturns an iterator over each run of `size` neighboring items of a slice.\n\nEach run is a `(ref (slice a))`. A slice shorter than `size` gives no run.\nIt panics when `size` is zero or negative. A vector passes as its slice.\n\n(-> (vec 1 2 3) (windows 2) count)\n=> 2\n\n(-> (vec 1) (windows 2) count)\n=> 0")
